The dynamic nature of Internet of Things (IoT) environments affects the long-term effectiveness of Machine Learning as a Service (MLaaS) compositions. IoT data streams often experience data distribution shifts, affecting the performance of the composition. Existing adaptive composition methods primarily rely on service replacement or re-composition, which may not always yield the expected performance because identifying suitable substitutes in dynamic environments is challenging and time-consuming. To address this, we propose a novel Test-Time Adaptive MLaaS composition framework for IoT environments. We develop a Composition-Aware Test-Time Adaptation (TTA) Trigger Mechanism to reduce false alarm ratios for reliable adaptation. Next, we develop a TTA-aware composability model to determine the composability of the adapted services with the existing composition. We then design a Service-Level TTA Model to perform TTA at the service level to retain composition performance. Finally, we design a Composition-Level TTA Model to enable adaptation at the composition level without affecting the overall composition. Experimental results on real-world datasets demonstrate its effectiveness over traditional adaptive approaches in reducing computational time and cost.
